1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,922
Payday hits, and for about 48 hours,

2
00:00:03,922 --> 00:00:05,097
you feel rich.

3
00:00:05,437 --> 00:00:07,029
Then rent, subscriptions,

4
00:00:07,029 --> 00:00:09,895
and one little impulse order take everything.

5
00:00:10,235 --> 00:00:12,746
And by the 20th, you're broke again.

6
00:00:13,086 --> 00:00:14,723
If that sounds familiar,

7
00:00:14,723 --> 00:00:16,222
this video is for you.

8
00:00:18,222 --> 00:00:20,011
Welcome to Broke to Balance,

9
00:00:20,011 --> 00:00:22,247
where we turn paychecks into plans.

10
00:00:22,587 --> 00:00:22,946
Today,

11
00:00:22,946 --> 00:00:24,803
you'll learn how to budget your

12
00:00:24,803 --> 00:00:26,661
paycheck using one simple rule,

13
00:00:26,661 --> 00:00:27,979
in under five minutes.

14
00:00:29,979 --> 00:00:30,671
Step one:

15
00:00:30,671 --> 00:00:33,976
budget your take-home pay, not your salary.

16
00:00:34,316 --> 00:00:36,388
Take-home is the money that actually

17
00:00:36,388 --> 00:00:37,941
lands in your bank account,

18
00:00:37,941 --> 00:00:39,495
after taxes and deductions.

19
00:00:39,835 --> 00:00:43,384
In this example, you earn $3,600 a month.

20
00:00:43,724 --> 00:00:46,690
After $600 in taxes and deductions,

21
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:49,263
$3,000 is yours to plan.

22
00:00:51,263 --> 00:00:53,040
Meet the 50/30/20 rule,

23
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:56,286
popularized by Elizabeth Warren and Amelia

24
00:00:56,286 --> 00:00:59,377
Warren Tyagi in the book All Your Worth.

25
00:00:59,717 --> 00:01:02,356
50% of your take-home goes to needs.

26
00:01:02,696 --> 00:01:04,389
30% goes to wants.

27
00:01:04,729 --> 00:01:07,630
And 20% goes to savings and debt payoff.

28
00:01:07,970 --> 00:01:13,277
On $3,000, that's $1,500, $900, and $600.

29
00:01:15,277 --> 00:01:17,896
Needs are the bills you can't skip:

30
00:01:17,896 --> 00:01:21,113
rent, groceries, utilities, transportation,

31
00:01:21,113 --> 00:01:22,161
and insurance.

32
00:01:22,501 --> 00:01:25,025
Add them up in a spreadsheet, like this.

33
00:01:25,365 --> 00:01:29,149
In our example, needs total $1,500,

34
00:01:29,149 --> 00:01:30,447
exactly 50%.

35
00:01:30,787 --> 00:01:33,348
If yours come in higher, don't panic.

36
00:01:33,348 --> 00:01:35,909
That's not failure, it's information,

37
00:01:35,909 --> 00:01:38,055
and it shows you where to trim.

38
00:01:40,055 --> 00:01:42,268
Wants are what make life fun:

39
00:01:42,268 --> 00:01:45,550
eating out, streaming, shopping, and trips.

40
00:01:45,890 --> 00:01:47,797
$900 is a real amount of fun,

41
00:01:47,797 --> 00:01:50,164
but only if you decide where it goes

42
00:01:50,164 --> 00:01:51,742
before the month starts.

43
00:01:52,082 --> 00:01:53,001
Here's the trick:

44
00:01:53,001 --> 00:01:54,893
give every want a name and a limit.

45
00:01:56,893 --> 00:01:59,668
The last 20% is what builds your balance.

46
00:02:00,008 --> 00:02:02,455
First, a starter emergency fund.

47
00:02:02,795 --> 00:02:04,999
Then, pay down high-interest debt.

48
00:02:05,339 --> 00:02:07,552
Then, invest for your future.

49
00:02:07,892 --> 00:02:12,606
Saving $600 a month adds up to $7,200 in one year.

50
00:02:12,946 --> 00:02:16,420
Invested for 5 years at an assumed 7%

51
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:19,519
average return (not a guarantee),

52
00:02:19,519 --> 00:02:22,430
it could grow to about $43,000.

53
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:24,709
That's the power of consistency.

54
00:02:27,309 --> 00:02:28,906
Three tips to make this stick.

55
00:02:29,246 --> 00:02:29,536
One:

56
00:02:29,536 --> 00:02:30,915
pay yourself first.

57
00:02:30,915 --> 00:02:33,092
Automate a transfer on payday,

58
00:02:33,092 --> 00:02:36,575
so your savings leave before you can spend them.

59
00:02:36,915 --> 00:02:37,179
Two:

60
00:02:37,179 --> 00:02:39,357
track it in a simple spreadsheet.

61
00:02:39,357 --> 00:02:41,601
10 minutes a week is all it takes.

62
00:02:41,941 --> 00:02:42,443
Three:

63
00:02:42,443 --> 00:02:45,205
adjust the rule to fit your life.

64
00:02:45,205 --> 00:02:47,799
If rent is high where you live,

65
00:02:47,799 --> 00:02:48,887
try 60/20/20.

66
00:02:48,887 --> 00:02:51,062
Progress beats perfection.

67
00:02:53,062 --> 00:02:54,253
Before we wrap up,

68
00:02:54,253 --> 00:02:57,030
three mistakes that quietly break budgets.

69
00:02:57,370 --> 00:02:58,111
Mistake one:

70
00:02:58,111 --> 00:03:00,272
budgeting your gross salary instead

71
00:03:00,272 --> 00:03:01,631
of your take-home pay.

72
00:03:01,631 --> 00:03:03,360
That makes every number look

73
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:04,904
bigger than it really is.

74
00:03:05,244 --> 00:03:06,135
Mistake two:

75
00:03:06,135 --> 00:03:08,140
forgetting irregular costs,

76
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:11,185
like car repairs, gifts, and annual fees.

77
00:03:11,185 --> 00:03:13,412
Set aside a little each month,

78
00:03:13,412 --> 00:03:15,418
so they don't surprise you.

79
00:03:15,758 --> 00:03:16,618
Mistake three:

80
00:03:16,618 --> 00:03:19,261
making your budget so strict that you quit.

81
00:03:19,261 --> 00:03:20,428
Leave room for fun,

82
00:03:20,428 --> 00:03:22,579
because a budget you can stick with

83
00:03:22,579 --> 00:03:24,423
beats a perfect one you can't.

84
00:03:26,423 --> 00:03:28,137
So, here's the recap.

85
00:03:28,477 --> 00:03:30,102
50% for needs.

86
00:03:30,442 --> 00:03:31,970
30% for wants.

87
00:03:32,310 --> 00:03:34,145
20% for future you.

88
00:03:34,485 --> 00:03:35,448
If this helped,

89
00:03:35,448 --> 00:03:37,375
subscribe to Broke to Balance,

90
00:03:37,375 --> 00:03:39,174
and tell me in the comments:

91
00:03:39,174 --> 00:03:41,229
which bucket is hardest for you?

92
00:03:41,569 --> 00:03:44,002
This video is for education only,

93
00:03:44,002 --> 00:03:45,993
and isn't financial advice.

94
00:03:45,993 --> 00:03:47,320
See you next time.

